{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And when a stranger sojourns with you, etc.</b> It uses the singular form <b>with you</b> and then reverted to say the plural form <b>in your land.</b> This is because the drawing down of converts comes about through the mediation of the righteous one, Abraham our father, peace be upon him, and also because of the purity of the Holy Land, whereby souls of the captive are drawn forth. Therefore it says <b>with you</b> in the singular, alluding to the righteous one — for not all of them are in the category of drawing down branches of holiness — and it says <b>in your land</b> to allude to the deed of the land, for it too partakes of this good influence." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes to the indwelling of the Shekhinah in this world with us, and HaShem commands from on high: ״כִּי יָגוּר אִתְּךָ גֵּר״. It was precise in saying ״אִתְּךָ״ after the manner of (Psalms 78:60) ״אֹהֶל שִׁכֵּן בָּאָדָם״. And it says <b>in your land</b> — this is the Holy Land, for there is the place of the Shekhinah, which left its supernal place and came to sojourn on earth. ״לֹא תוֹנוּ אוֹתוֹ״ — for there is no oppression like the oppression of the Shekhinah. And its saying <b>as one born among you,</b> etc., is to warn concerning the converts, etc., who come to become Jews."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:38.823173237Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}